    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">1. Understanding Header Anatomy</h2>
      <p>
        In wood platform framing, every rough opening interrupts vertical wall studs. To prevent ceiling joists and roof trusses from sagging, a horizontal beam called a <strong>header</strong> spans across the top of the opening. The header is supported on each side by vertical <strong>jack studs (also called trimmers)</strong>, which transfer the downward load to the bottom sill plate. King studs run alongside the jacks from plate to plate to provide lateral stiffness.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">2. IRC Table R602.7(1) Header Span Matrix</h2>
      <p>
        The International Residential Code specifies maximum header spans based on the building width and whether the wall supports a roof only, or a roof plus one or two stories above. For a typical 28-foot wide single-story house with a 30 PSF ground snow load, the maximum spans for #2 Douglas Fir / Southern Pine are:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-xl border border-gray-200 shadow-xs text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-800 font-bold border-b border-gray-200">
            <tr>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Header Size</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Roof Only (Max Span)</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Roof + 1 Center Floor</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Required Jack Studs</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700">
            <tr>
