      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">3. When Must You Switch to Engineered LVL?</h2>
      <p>
        Standard dimension lumber tops out at a 2x12. Under typical residential roof loads, even a double 2x12 cannot safely span more than 8 to 9 feet. Therefore, for standard <strong>16-foot double-car garage doors</strong> or 12-foot multi-panel sliding glass patio doors, sawn lumber is code-prohibited. You must use engineered Laminated Veneer Lumber (LVL) or steel flitch plates.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">4. Jack Stud Bearing Requirements</h2>
      <p>
        A common framing mistake is installing a heavy header on only one jack stud per side. IRC Table R602.7(1) requires a minimum 1.5 inches of end bearing (one 2x4 jack) for openings up to 5 feet. For spans exceeding 5 to 6 feet, you must install <strong>two jack studs (3 inches of solid end bearing)</strong> at each end of the header. For 16-foot garage openings carrying roof trusses, many structural engineers specify three jack studs (4.5 inches bearing).
      </p>
